WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.640
Greetings to all of you Fellow Scholars

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:05.780
out there this is Two Minute Papers where

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.910
I explain awesome research works, a

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.540
couple minutes at a time.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.460
You know, I wish someone explain to me in simple terms

00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:15.530
what's going on in genetics, biology and

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.280
just about every field of scientific research.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.780
There are tons of wonderful

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.860
works coming every day that we don't

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.960
know about, and I'm trying my best here

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.880
to bring it to you the simplest way I

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.250
possibly can. So you know,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.380
researchers are people and physics

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.970
research at the Hadron Collider

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.070
basically means that people smash atoms

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:34.790
together.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.950
Well, computer graphics people also like

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.390
to have some fun and write simulation

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.880
programs to smash together a variety of

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.890
objects in slow motion.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.810
However, even though most of these

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.700
simulations look pretty good

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.680
they are physically not correct as many

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.660
effects are neglected such as simulating

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.300
plasticity, bending, stiffness, stretching,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.060
energies and many others.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.040
But unfortunately, these are too expensive to

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.890
compute in high resolution, unless you

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.510
have some tricks up the sleeve.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.489
Researchers at UC Berkeley have managed

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:06.170
to crack this nut by creating an

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.240
algorithm that uses more computational

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.370
resources only around regions where

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.360
cracks are likely to happen.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.420
This new technique enables the simulation of

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.729
tearing for a variety of materials like

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:20.720
cork, foils, metals, vinyl and it also

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.360
yields physically correct results for glass.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.540
Here's an example of a beaten-up rubber

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:27.229
sheet from their simulation program

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:29.930
compared to a real-world photograph.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.850
It's really awesome that you can do something

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.490
on your computer in a virtual world that

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.680
has something to do with reality.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.040
It is impossible to get used to this feeling,

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.140
it's so amazing. And what's even better

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.450
since it is really difficult to know in

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.400
advance how the cracks would exactly look

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.799
like they have also enhanced the directability

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:51.259
of the simulation, so artists

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:53.180
could change things up a bit to achieve

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.640
a desired artistic effect.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.619
In this example they have managed to avoid

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.140
tearing a duck in two by weakening the

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.480
paths around them.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.620
Bravo!

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.160
Thanks for watching and if you

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.320
liked this series just hit the like and

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.240
subscribe buttons below the video to

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.880
become a member of our growing club of scholars.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.520
Thanks, and I'll see you next time!

